  <h1>Common Overtime Misconceptions</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by our editorial team. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>Both workers and employers frequently get overtime wrong. Some of the misconceptions favor employers (workers leave money on the table); some favor workers (workers pursue claims that won't succeed). This guide collects the misunderstandings we see most often, with the actual rule, the regulatory or case-law source, and a practical note on what to do.</p>

  <h2>1. "Salaried employees can't get overtime"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong.</strong> Being paid a salary doesn't automatically make you exempt. Exemption under the FLSA requires BOTH (a) a salary at or above the federal threshold (currently $684/week, with regulatory updates) AND (b) meeting the duties test for executive, administrative, professional, computer, outside sales, or another specifically defined category. Many salaried employees are still entitled to overtime because they fail one of these prongs.</p>
  <p>The duties test is fact-specific. For the executive exemption, the worker's primary duty must be management of the enterprise or a recognized department, the worker must customarily and regularly direct the work of at least two other full-time employees, and the worker must have the authority to hire or fire (or whose recommendations on these are given particular weight). A "manager" who spends most of their time doing the same line work as the people they supervise typically fails the primary duty prong.</p>

  <h2>2. "My boss called me a manager so I'm exempt"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong.</strong> Job titles don't determine exemption. What you actually do does. If your primary duty is non-managerial work (operating a register, taking orders, processing data, cleaning), you may be entitled to overtime even with a "manager" or "supervisor" title.</p>
  <p>Job title misclassification is one of the most-litigated areas of wage-and-hour law. The standard practical question: in a typical 40-hour week, what percentage of your time is genuinely managerial (hiring, evaluating, scheduling, supervising, budget responsibility) versus production work? If less than half is managerial, the executive exemption almost certainly fails.</p>

  <h2>3. "Weekend hours always count as overtime"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong (federally).</strong> Federal overtime is based on hours over 40 in a workweek, not on which day of the week. If you work 32 hours Monday through Friday and 8 hours Saturday, that's 40 total &mdash; no federal overtime. Some union contracts, a few state industries, and Massachusetts (historically, for retail) have required premium pay for weekend hours, but the federal default has no weekend rule.</p>
  <p>This goes the other way too: if you only worked one day this week (a 12-hour Sunday), there's no federal overtime even though it's a "weekend day." Federal overtime is hours-based, not day-based.</p>

  <h2>4. "My employer can give me comp time instead of overtime pay"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong for private-sector employees.</strong> The FLSA does not allow private employers to substitute compensatory time off ("comp time") for overtime pay. Even if you agree to it, even if the comp time is at the 1.5x rate, even if your employer calls it a "flex schedule" &mdash; the substitution is unlawful in the private sector.</p>
  <p>The narrow exception: public-sector employees (state and local government) can sometimes receive comp time at 1.5 hours per overtime hour, with restrictions including a maximum accrual cap and a requirement that the agreement is part of a collective bargaining agreement or other appropriate authorization.</p>

  <h2>5. "If I agreed to work without overtime, that's binding"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong.</strong> Overtime rights under the FLSA cannot be waived by private agreement. Even a signed contract saying you waive overtime is unenforceable. Employers sometimes ask employees to sign these anyway &mdash; the document is meaningless for FLSA purposes.</p>
  <p>The same is true for "I'll work for less than minimum wage to gain experience" agreements. Minimum wage and overtime are minimum standards set by statute; private parties can't contract around them.</p>

  <h2>6. "My break time is unpaid so I can't claim it as work"</h2>
  <p><strong>Depends.</strong> The general rule under 29 CFR &sect; 785: short breaks (5&ndash;20 minutes) are typically compensable and count as work. Bona fide meal breaks (30+ minutes where you're completely relieved of duties) can be unpaid &mdash; but the "completely relieved of duties" qualifier is critical.</p>
  <p>If you were actually working during your "lunch" &mdash; checking emails, answering calls, monitoring equipment, supervising customers &mdash; that time is compensable. Many wage-and-hour cases turn on whether interrupted meal breaks were genuinely compensable working time that should have been counted toward overtime thresholds.</p>

  <h2>7. "Work I did from home doesn't count"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong.</strong> If you were authorized or expected to do work, the time counts toward your hours &mdash; including remote work. Off-the-clock work that your employer knew or should have known about must be compensated. This includes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Answering emails after hours</li>
    <li>Taking work calls during personal time</li>
    <li>Logging into systems remotely to handle issues</li>
    <li>Preparing reports or materials from home</li>
    <li>Continuing client work after leaving the office</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The "should have known" standard means an employer cannot avoid liability by formally telling you not to work after hours while in practice expecting you to be responsive. If after-hours email responses are part of the actual job, that time is compensable regardless of any written "no off-the-clock work" policy.</p>

  <h2>8. "My pay was correct because they paid 'time and a half' for my overtime hours"</h2>
  <p><strong>Maybe wrong.</strong> Time-and-a-half is on your REGULAR RATE, which includes non-discretionary bonuses, commissions, and shift differentials &mdash; not just your base hourly wage. If you receive any of these supplemental payments and your overtime was calculated only on base wage, you've likely been underpaid.</p>
  <p>This is the single most common source of unpaid overtime we see in practice. A typical scenario: you earn $20/hour base, you receive a $200 quarterly attendance bonus, and you work 50 hours in a week within that quarter. Your overtime computation has to retroactively include the bonus in the regular rate for the workweeks covered by the bonus, which means the employer owes you slightly more than the simple time-and-a-half-on-base-wage number.</p>

  <h2>9. "If I sue, I'll just get my back wages"</h2>
  <p><strong>You can often get more.</strong> Under federal FLSA, willful violations entitle you to "liquidated damages" equal to the unpaid wages &mdash; so double back pay. The standard for willful is whether the employer knew or showed reckless disregard for whether its conduct was prohibited; many wage violations meet that bar.</p>
  <p>Plus, attorney's fees are recoverable in most cases (FLSA &sect; 16(b)). This fee-shifting provision is what makes contingency-fee representation economically viable for wage-and-hour attorneys; the fees come from the employer on a successful judgment or settlement, not out of the worker's recovery.</p>
  <p>Many state laws add further multipliers and penalties. California's PAGA provides for civil penalties per pay period per violation that can dwarf the underlying back wages. New York has its own statutory liquidated damages.</p>

  <h2>10. "Independent contractors aren't entitled to overtime"</h2>
  <p><strong>True &mdash; but misclassification is widespread.</strong> Genuine independent contractors aren't entitled to overtime under the FLSA. But just being called an "independent contractor" in your contract doesn't make you one; the determination is based on the economic-realities test (or for California, the ABC test under AB 5).</p>
  <p>Workers misclassified as independent contractors are often entitled to recovery of unpaid overtime, minimum wage, and other employment-law protections going back several years. If your day-to-day work looks indistinguishable from an employee's (set hours, direct supervision, employer-provided tools, no real ability to work for competitors), the contractor label may be wrong.</p>

  <h2>11. "I waited too long; the statute of limitations has run"</h2>
  <p><strong>Maybe not.</strong> Federal statute of limitations: 2 years from the date of the violation (each underpaid paycheck is a separate violation that starts its own clock), extended to 3 years for willful violations. State statutes range from 2 to 6 years (NY).</p>
  <p>Even if some workweeks are time-barred, more recent ones may not be. Don't assume the entire claim is dead because you waited &mdash; check the math for each pay period.</p>

  <h2>12. "If I complain, I'll be fired"</h2>
  <p><strong>Retaliation is illegal but the practical risk is real.</strong> Federal FLSA &sect; 15(a)(3) and state laws make retaliation for wage complaints illegal, with remedies including reinstatement, back pay, and additional damages. But the practical reality is that proving retaliation is harder when the worker's performance has been questioned and easier when the timing is suspicious.</p>
  <p>If you anticipate retaliation, the practical steps are: document your performance and any positive feedback before complaining; file the complaint in writing with a clear date; preserve evidence of any change in how you're treated after; consider filing first with a wage agency (which can investigate before the employer is formally notified, although discovery typically occurs at some point).</p>

  <h2>13. "Overtime can be paid in the next pay period to spread it out"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong.</strong> Overtime must be paid on the regular pay date for the pay period in which it was earned. Employers cannot defer overtime to a later pay period to manage cash flow. Late payment of overtime is a separate FLSA violation.</p>
  <p>Some bonuses paid quarterly or annually <em>can</em> be allocated back to the workweeks that earned them and require recalculation of overtime owed for those weeks &mdash; that's different from deferring overtime payment itself. The recalculation must occur and any additional overtime owed must be paid promptly once the bonus is determined.</p>

  <h2>14. "Tipped employees don't get full overtime"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong.</strong> Tipped employees are entitled to overtime computed on the full minimum wage (or higher regular rate), NOT on the reduced cash wage. The employer can still take the tip credit toward the resulting hourly rate, but the regular rate for overtime purposes cannot be less than the full federal or state minimum.</p>
  <p>This is one of the most-miscalculated overtime scenarios because the math is counterintuitive. The result is that tipped employees should see a noticeable bump in pay for overtime hours even though their base cash wage may be very low.</p>

  <h2>Bottom line</h2>
  <p>Most overtime misconceptions stem from the same underlying intuition: "this is what my employer/contract/HR rep told me, so it must be right." Wage-and-hour law is built on minimum standards that can't be contracted around or waived. When in doubt, the FLSA and state wage statutes set the floor, regardless of what the offer letter, employee handbook, or paystub format suggests.</p>
  <p>For the underlying mechanics, see <a href="/guides/how-overtime-is-calculated/">How overtime is calculated</a>. For state-specific rules, see <a href="/guides/state-overtime-laws-explained/">State overtime laws explained</a>. If you've identified a real underpayment, see <a href="/guides/what-to-do-if-your-employer-wont-pay-overtime/">What to do if your employer won't pay overtime</a>.</p>
